# Chapter 12. RX: the Machine's REXX

/STARTUP.BAT is a list of commands, and a list of commands is enough right up
to the moment something has to be decided. RX is the answer to that: a REXX
for this machine, in the Amiga's tradition rather than the mainframe's.

```
RX HELLO
```

runs HELLO.RX. So does typing HELLO on its own, because the shell's rule for
an unknown word -- try it as a program on disk -- takes a script as readily as
a .prg. The scripts that come with the machine are in /LANG/RX.

## Why REXX, and what ARexx was

ARexx on the Amiga was two things at once. It was the REXX language --
readable, typeless, and forgiving in the way a scripting language for people
who are not programmers has to be. And it was the port system: every serious
Amiga program published a port, so ten lines of REXX could drive a paint
program and a terminal in the same script. The second half is why anybody
remembers it.

Both halves are here. The language is demo/rexx.c, 37 KB loaded at $0800
because it wants nearly the whole program area, and it is a real one: strings
and whole numbers, PARSE with its templates, DO in all its forms, SELECT,
internal functions with PROCEDURE EXPOSE and recursion, compound (stem)
variables, SIGNAL, and about fifty built-in functions.

The thing that makes it glue is REXX's oldest idea: a clause the interpreter
does not recognise is not an error, it is a command, sent to whatever the
current environment is.

```
ADDRESS COMMAND
'DIR APPS'
IF RC = 0 THEN SAY 'that worked'
```

## The three environments

```
ADDRESS  Sends the line to
COMMAND  K/OS's own shell -- exactly as if you had typed it. The result comes
         back in RC
TUBE     the co-processor's shell, with its output brought back in RESULT
name     a program's port (below)
```

## Ports: the file is the mailbox

The Amiga's ports were message ports between tasks that were all running at
once. This machine runs one program at a time, so that arrangement could not
be copied -- and the substitute is the obvious one: a file.

A script writes its request to /LANG/RX/MAIL.CMD, SWAPs to the program with
that file as its one argument, and reads the reply from /LANG/RX/MAIL.RPL: a
result code on the first line and the text after it. A program joins the
scheme by honouring one argument, which is about twenty lines of work.

CHESS is the worked example, and it plays a whole game this way: NEW, MOVE,
GO, LEVEL, BOARD, FEN and STATUS. Its position lives in /APPS/CHESS/PORT.GAM,
because a program swapped in starts fresh every time and the file is its
memory. CHESS.RX in /LANG/RX plays against the engine and draws the board
itself from what comes back.

## What is in /LANG/RX

A language with nothing written in it proves nothing, so:

```
HELLO.RX     the smallest one
MACHINE.RX   the machine reading its own registers
HOSTINFO.RX  the host asked about itself, over the Tube
WC.RX        a word count
SUMFILE.RX   a column adder
GUESS.RX     a guessing game
WUMPUS.RX    Hunt the Wumpus, in 150 lines
CHESS.RX     a game of chess played through the port
```

> Games are good tests. Wumpus alone found three real faults in the
> interpreter on the day it was written: EXPOSE of a stem was assigning it,
> which silently threw away every member of the caller's map; LEAVE stopped at
> the first END it met rather than the loop's, so leaving from inside a SELECT
> went nowhere; and a plain DO group was counting as a loop. None of them
> would have shown up in a script that only ran commands in order.

> One thing to know about running programs from a script. A program started by
> the shell loads at $6000 -- through the middle of the interpreter. So RX
> asks the filesystem whether the first word of a command names a file: a
> built-in shell command runs directly and its output stays on the screen, and
> a program is swapped in and out around the interpreter. That is also why RX
> uses SWAP -k (Chapter 2, The Shell): the ordinary SWAP would restore the
> screen and throw away what the program had just printed for the script to
> read.

